This study presents the initial measurement of the differential decay rate for the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ process as a function of the dimensionless variable $w=(p_{B_s}/p_{D_s*})[(m_{B_s}^2+m_{D_s*}^2)- (m_{B_s}^2-m_{D_s*}^2)]$. The experiment utilized a dataset corresponding to an integrated luminosity of 3.2 fb$^{-1}$ from $pp$ collisions recorded by LHCb. The recorded decays were reconstructed via two distinct channels: $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ followed by $D_s^{*-}ightarrow D_s^{-}\gamma$ and $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ followed by $D_s^{*-}ightarrow K^{-}K^{+}\pi^{-}$. The collected differential decay rate aligns with the predictions derived from constant form factor parametrisations when used for fitting. Specifically, the 95\% confidence level limits for the ratios $|f_2/f_0|$ and $|f_3/f_0|$ are determined to be 0.14 and 0.17, respectively. These results support a parametrisation where the dominant term is constant, consistent with the form factor extracted from the $B^0ightarrow D^{*0}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay. The analysed dataset comprises $1.74\pm 0.04(\mathrm{stat})\pm 0.10(\mathrm{syst})	imes 10^6$ events. The ultimate precision is constrained by the small branching fraction of the $D_s^{*-}ightarrow D_s^{-}\gamma$ transition. Ultimately, this measurement serves as a stringent test of Heavy Quark Effective Theory and Constrained Semi-Empirical models, while providing an additional dataset for the global fit within the Standard Model framework.
